In the previous post, we took the monumental step of ripping out Create React App and migrating to Vite. Our development server now starts instantly, and hot module replacement (HMR) is a breeze. But that was only step one of the Stack Modernization series (Sprint 5).

With a lightning-fast build toolchain in place, it was time to tackle the code itself. The portfolio was originally written in pure, loosely-typed JavaScript. As the codebase grew—especially with the introduction of a custom markdown CMS, a complex theme switcher, and automated DynamoDB CDC pipelines—the lack of type safety became a massive liability.

In this post, I will break down how I migrated the entire legacy JavaScript codebase to strict TypeScript, diving into domain modeling, advanced React typings, and securing the API layer.



### The Strict Mode Deep Dive

The easiest way to migrate to TypeScript is to enable `allowJs` and slowly rename files from `.js` to `.ts`. The *right* way to migrate, however, is to rip the band-aid off. 

I updated my `tsconfig.json` and enabled `"strict": true`. This single line flips on a suite of rigorous type-checking flags, including `noImplicitAny`, `strictNullChecks`, and `strictBindCallApply`. 

Instantly, my editor lit up like a Christmas tree. Hundreds of red squiggles appeared. Variables that were implicitly `any` were flagged, optional object properties that could be `undefined` were throwing errors, and function parameters demanded explicit types. 

While overwhelming at first, this strictness is exactly what I wanted. It forced me to map out the exact shape of my data.



### Domain Modeling: The Single Source of Truth

The first step to resolving the cascade of `any` errors was establishing a central domain model. I created a new file at `src/types/index.ts` to act as the single source of truth for the application's core entities.

Previously, passing a "theme" or "blog" object around meant blindly hoping the receiving component knew what properties existed. Now, I defined rigid interfaces:

```typescript
export interface Theme {
  name: "light" | "dark" | "system";
  body: string;
  text: string;
  secondaryText: string;
  accentColor: string;
}

export interface Author {
  name: string;
  avatar: string;
}

export interface Blog {
  slug: string;
  title: string;
  summary: string;
  content?: string; // Optional because previews don't fetch full markdown
  publishDate: string;
  coverImage: string;
  readTime: string;
  tags: string[];
  author: Author;
}
```

By exporting these interfaces, I could finally type the raw data coming from AWS and enforce consistency across all React components.



### Advanced React Typing

With the domain models in place, I began renaming components to `.tsx` and typing their props. In the legacy JavaScript code, I used `prop-types` (or sometimes nothing at all). In TypeScript, this becomes dramatically more powerful.

For functional components, I used `React.FC` (Function Component) and passed in a generic interface for the props:

```typescript
interface BlogCardProps {
  blog: Blog;
  theme: Theme;
  onClick: (slug: string) => void;
}

const BlogCard: React.FC<BlogCardProps> = ({ blog, theme, onClick }) => {
  // TS now knows exactly what `blog.title` and `theme.accentColor` are!
  return (
    <div onClick={() => onClick(blog.slug)}>
      <h2>{blog.title}</h2>
    </div>
  );
};
```

Typing `useRef` was another interesting challenge. A standard ref for a DOM element needs to explicitly state the element type, initializing with `null`:

```typescript
const containerRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
```

TypeScript then enforces `strictNullChecks`, meaning I couldn't just call `containerRef.current.scrollIntoView()`. I had to explicitly check if `.current` existed first, preventing runtime crashes.



### Securing the API Layer

Perhaps the most critical part of the migration was typing the data boundary—where the frontend meets the AWS backend. `fetch` API responses are inherently `any` typed. You can tell TypeScript a response is a `Blog[]`, but it won't actually validate it at runtime.

To bridge this gap safely, I created a generic API client wrapper. It takes a Type parameter `<T>` and casts the parsed JSON. 

```typescript
async function fetchFromApi<T>(endpoint: string): Promise<T> {
  const response = await fetch(`${import.meta.env.VITE_CUSTOM_API_URL}${endpoint}`);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`API Error: ${response.status}`);
  }

  return (await response.json()) as T;
}

// Usage:
const blogs = await fetchFromApi<Blog[]>('/blogs');
```

While this still relies on a type assertion (`as T`), it centralizes the logic. If I ever decide to add runtime validation (like Zod), I only have to update this one generic function.



### Ambient Declarations and Static Assets

The final hurdle in the Vite + TypeScript migration was static assets. When importing an SVG or PNG in a `.tsx` file, TypeScript threw an error because it didn't know what a `.svg` module was.

To fix this, I relied on Vite's ambient declarations. By creating a `vite-env.d.ts` file in the `src` directory with a specific reference, I instructed TypeScript to inherit Vite's built-in client types:

```typescript
/// <reference types="vite/client" />
```

This single line magically resolved all import errors for assets and properly typed the `import.meta.env` object.



### The Payoff

Refactoring a legacy codebase to strict TypeScript is tedious, but the ROI is astronomical. As I refactored, TypeScript caught dozens of silent bugs: misnamed object keys, missing null checks, and incorrect prop drilling. 

With Vite providing instant builds and TypeScript providing instant type safety, the developer experience of this portfolio is now truly world-class.

In the next post, we will tackle the final (and most daunting) phase of Sprint 5: converting over 35 legacy React Class Components into modern, hook-based Functional Components!




**Read Previous:** [Modernizing the Build Toolchain: From Create React App to Vite](/blogs/stack-migration-p1-cra-to-vite)  
**Read Next:** [React Classes to Hooks at Scale: How I Converted 35+ Components](/blogs/stack-migration-p3-classes-to-hooks)

