Welcome to Sprint 3 of my portfolio redesign journey! In the previous sprints, we tackled the foundations: a robust, state-driven dark mode (Sprint 1) and a cinematic, responsive hero section (Sprint 2). Now, it was time to address a critical flaw in the old design: the landing page felt a bit empty once you scrolled past the intro. 

A portfolio shouldn't just be a digital resume; it should be an interactive hub that surfaces your best work immediately. To reduce friction and increase engagement, I decided to pull my two biggest passion areas directly onto the home page: my technical blog and my travel photography. 

Here is how I built the `FeaturedBlogs` and `TravelTeaser` components, reused existing API logic, and wove a secondary travel identity into a strictly tech-focused portfolio.



### The Problem: Content Friction

In the original design, if a recruiter or engineer landed on `amrit.cloud`, they had to explicitly navigate to the `/blogs` tab to see my writing. That's one click too many. If you want people to read your work, you have to put it right in front of them.

The solution was to build a `FeaturedBlogs` component that grabs the 3 most recent articles and displays them in a sleek CSS grid right below the hero section.

### Reusing the API Client

One of the benefits of having a clean architecture is reusability. I already had a `fetchBlogs()` function in `src/utils/apiClient.js` that pulls the full list of blogs from my AWS API Gateway (which queries DynamoDB). 

Instead of writing a new backend endpoint to "get top 3 blogs", I simply reused the existing client and sliced the array on the frontend. Since DynamoDB pagination isn't strictly necessary for a blog with ~20 posts, fetching the full array and slicing it is perfectly performant.

```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { fetchBlogs } from '../../utils/apiClient';
import BlogCard from '../blogCard/BlogCard';

export default function FeaturedBlogs({ theme }) {
  const [featured, setFeatured] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    const getFeaturedBlogs = async () => {
      try {
        const data = await fetchBlogs();
        // Grab the 3 most recent blogs
        setFeatured(data.slice(0, 3));
      } catch (error) {
        console.error("Failed to load featured blogs", error);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    };
    getFeaturedBlogs();
  }, []);

  // ... render logic
}
```

### Designing for State: Skeleton Loaders

Because the `FeaturedBlogs` component relies on a network request, there's an inherent delay before the content renders. If you just leave it blank, the layout will abruptly "jump" when the data arrives, causing a jarring layout shift (a metric Google Lighthouse heavily penalizes).

To fix this, I implemented a skeleton loading state. While `loading` is true, the component renders three pulsing placeholder cards that perfectly match the dimensions of the actual `BlogCard`.

```css
/* A simple CSS pulsing animation for the skeleton loader */
.skeleton-card {
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, #2d2d2d 25%, #3d3d3d 50%, #2d2d2d 75%);
  background-size: 200% 100%;
  animation: pulse 1.5s infinite linear;
  border-radius: 8px;
  height: 300px;
}

@keyframes pulse {
  0% { background-position: 200% 0; }
  100% { background-position: -200% 0; }
}
```



### Introducing the Travel Teaser

I'm an engineer, but I'm also a traveler. Having grown up in Nepal, the Himalayas are deeply ingrained in my identity. I wanted the portfolio to reflect that dual nature without confusing the user.

I created the `TravelTeaser` component. It sits right below the blog preview and acts as a gateway to a completely different side of my life. 

#### Design Choices: Split Cards and Destination Chips
The teaser is divided into a clean, 2-column flexbox layout (which stacks vertically on mobile). 
1. **The Nepal Side**: Focuses on Himalayan trekking, using a dark, moody background with destination chips like "Annapurna Base Camp", "Mustang", and "Tilicho Lake".
2. **The USA Side**: Focuses on motorcycle trips and cross-country travel, utilizing a slightly different visual tone.

To tie it all together, I embedded an inline SVG mountain silhouette as the background of the teaser section. By making it an inline SVG, I could easily target its `fill` property with CSS to make it theme-aware (soft gray in light mode, deep charcoal in dark mode).

### Data-Driven UI

To keep the `TravelTeaser` component clean, I extracted the destination lists and copy into the centralized `src/portfolio.js` file (which acts as the localized CMS for the entire site).

```javascript
// src/portfolio.js
export const travelData = {
  title: "Beyond the Keyboard",
  subtitle: "When I'm not architecting cloud systems, I'm usually exploring the mountains.",
  nepalDestinations: ["Annapurna Base Camp", "Tilicho Lake", "Gosaikunda", "Mustang"],
  usaDestinations: ["Pacific Coast Highway", "Yellowstone", "Grand Canyon"],
  // ...
};
```
By passing `travelData` down as props, the React components remain purely presentational, making them much easier to test and maintain.



### Wrapping Up Sprint 3

With the Blog Preview and Travel Teaser merged to `main`, the landing page finally feels like a complete story. It opens with a strong technical introduction, proves that technical expertise with immediate blog content, and then humanizes the experience with a glimpse into my travels.

But where does that "Explore Travel" button actually go? That brings us to Sprint 4, where we'll build out a dedicated `/travel` route and overhaul the site's footer to match the new aesthetic!



**Read Previous:** [Redesigning the Hero Section (Sprint 2)](/blogs/redesign-s2-hero-section)  
**Read Next:** [Building the Travel Page and Redesigning the Footer (Sprint 4)](/blogs/redesign-s4-travel-page-footer)

