This post kicks off a new multi part series detailing the comprehensive redesign of my serverless portfolio. Up until now, this technical blog series has focused primarily on the AWS backend from Lambda APIs and DynamoDB schemas to Cognito authentication. Now, we are pivoting to the frontend.

One of the most requested features for any modern site is a robust dark mode. When I started the first sprint of this redesign, my goal was not just to add a toggle. I wanted a state driven, three mode theme switcher (Light, System, Dark) that respects the OS preference of the user out of the box and persists seamlessly. 

Let us walk through how I migrated from a hardcoded theme export to a dynamic, persistent theme system using React and local storage.

```text
+-------------+         +------------------+         +--------------------+
| OS Settings | ------> | window.matchMedia| ------> | React State (App)  |
+-------------+         +------------------+         +--------------------+
                                 ^                            |
                                 |                            v
                          +-------------+            +--------------------+
                          | localStorage| <--------- | ThemeSwitcher UI   |
                          +-------------+            +--------------------+
```

### The Problem with Hardcoded Themes

In the legacy architecture of my portfolio, the theme was defined in a central configuration file and exported as a static constant. This constant was imported directly by components across the app. While this made it easy to style the site initially, it made dynamic switching impossible without a full page reload.

To implement a true dark mode, the theme needed to become a piece of global state that could flow down the component tree. Since the application relies heavily on styled components, the ThemeProvider was already in place, but it needed to be fed a dynamic object rather than a static one.

### Building the Three Mode Logic

I wanted three distinct options: Light, System, and Dark.

The System option is crucial. It reads the operating system color scheme preference via a media query. If the user changes their OS from light to dark, the site should instantly react.

Here is how I structured the core logic in my main App component:

```javascript
import React, { Component } from 'react';
import { ThemeProvider } from 'styled-components';
import { lightTheme, darkTheme } from './theme';

class App extends Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    // 1. Initialize state from local storage or default to system
    const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem('themeMode') || 'system';
    this.state = {
      themeMode: savedTheme,
      resolvedTheme: this.resolveTheme(savedTheme)
    };
  }

  resolveTheme = (mode) => {
    if (mode === 'dark') return darkTheme;
    if (mode === 'light') return lightTheme;

    // System mode logic
    if (window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches) {
      return darkTheme;
    }
    return lightTheme;
  };

  handleThemeChange = (newMode) => {
    // 2. Save preference and update state
    localStorage.setItem('themeMode', newMode);
    this.setState({
      themeMode: newMode,
      resolvedTheme: this.resolveTheme(newMode)
    });
  };

  render() {
    return (
      <ThemeProvider theme={this.state.resolvedTheme}>
        <Main 
          themeMode={this.state.themeMode} 
          onThemeChange={this.handleThemeChange} 
        />
      </ThemeProvider>
    );
  }
}
```

By placing this in the root class component, I could store the user preference in local storage and initialize the state on the very first render, avoiding any ugly flashes of unstyled content.

### Making it Reactive to OS Changes

A static check on load is not enough. If a user has the System option selected and their OS automatically switches to dark mode at sunset, the application needs to catch that event dynamically.

In the component lifecycle methods, I attach an event listener directly to the match media query:

```javascript
  componentDidMount() {
    if (window.matchMedia) {
      this.mediaQuery = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)');
      this.mediaQuery.addEventListener('change', this.handleSystemPreferenceChange);
    }
  }

  componentWillUnmount() {
    if (this.mediaQuery) {
      this.mediaQuery.removeEventListener('change', this.handleSystemPreferenceChange);
    }
  }

  handleSystemPreferenceChange = () => {
    // Only update if the user is actively using the system theme
    if (this.state.themeMode === 'system') {
      this.setState({
        resolvedTheme: this.resolveTheme('system')
      });
    }
  };
```

When the OS preference changes, the app re evaluates the system theme and updates the ThemeProvider, pushing the new colors down to every styled component instantly.

### Designing the Dark Palette

A common mistake in dark mode design is using pure black. It is harsh on the eyes and creates excessive contrast. Instead, I drew inspiration from the rich navies and grays used in professional developer tools like GitHub.

My dark theme palette uses a deep gray blue for the body background, with a bright and accessible blue as an accent color for active states and links. This ensures that whether someone is reading a technical blog post or checking out my upcoming travel blog entries, the reading experience remains comfortable.

### The Component UI

For the desktop view, I designed a three segment pill layout. It uses a sliding highlight indicator that transitions smoothly between the Light, System, and Dark segments. 

Here is a simplified look at how the ThemeSwitcher component passes the selected value back up the tree:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import './ThemeSwitcher.css';

const ThemeSwitcher = ({ currentMode, onThemeChange }) => {
  return (
    <div className="theme-switcher-container">
      <button 
        className={currentMode === 'light' ? 'active' : ''} 
        onClick={() => onThemeChange('light')}
      >
        Light
      </button>
      <button 
        className={currentMode === 'system' ? 'active' : ''} 
        onClick={() => onThemeChange('system')}
      >
        System
      </button>
      <button 
        className={currentMode === 'dark' ? 'active' : ''} 
        onClick={() => onThemeChange('dark')}
      >
        Dark
      </button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default ThemeSwitcher;
```

The switcher is fully accessible, implementing the correct ARIA roles and supporting keyboard navigation. It is the small details, like ensuring focus outlines are visible and color contrast meets WCAG standards, that elevate a project from a personal site to a professional showcase.

Next up in this redesign series, we will be completely overhauling the hero section to make a stronger first impression. Stay tuned.

