I spent today doing two things that seem unrelated at first but are actually deeply connected: fixing a broken deployment caused by a SonarCloud code quality gate, and redesigning the hero section of my portfolio. Both are part of the same ongoing landing page redesign series, and together they taught me something I keep relearning as a solo developer working on a real serverless portfolio: every shortcut catches up with you, usually at the worst moment.

**The morning started with a broken deploy**

After merging Sprint 1 (the three mode theme switcher), the CI/CD pipeline failed on the production build step. The error was not immediately obvious:

```text
./src/App.js
  Line 42:5:  Expected an assignment or function call and instead saw an expression  no unused expressions
  Line 50:5:  Expected an assignment or function call and instead saw an expression  no unused expressions
```

What happened? I had used optional chaining to call `addEventListener` and `removeEventListener` on a `MediaQueryList` object:

```javascript
this.mediaQuery?.addEventListener("change", this.handleSystemPreferenceChange);
```

This is perfectly valid modern JavaScript. SonarCloud even recommended it as a cleaner pattern than the `if (x && x.method)` form. But the older version of Create React App used in this project bundles an ESLint version that does not fully understand optional chaining on function calls. It saw the code as a no op expression, not a method call, and refused to compile.

The fix was straightforward. I reverted those specific lines to the explicit `if` guard pattern that the older tooling understands.

```javascript
// Before (SonarCloud preferred but older CRA incompatible)
this.mediaQuery?.addEventListener("change", this.handleSystemPreferenceChange);

// After (ESLint compatible with older CRA)
if (this.mediaQuery && this.mediaQuery.addEventListener) {
  this.mediaQuery.addEventListener("change", this.handleSystemPreferenceChange);
}
```

This is exactly the kind of tooling friction you encounter when you are on a legacy build setup. The fix is in the code today, but the real fix is Sprint 5 (migrating to Vite), which eliminates the legacy OpenSSL provider workaround and the entire CRA dependency.

**Improving test coverage to pass the quality gate**

Before the build failure, SonarCloud had also flagged that App.js test coverage was only 76 percent, below the required 80 percent threshold. The original test suite had a single smoke test:

```javascript
it("renders without crashing", () => {
  const div = document.createElement("div");
  ReactDOM.render(<App />, div);
  ReactDOM.unmountComponentAtNode(div);
});
```

That one test covers the happy path but leaves all the interesting logic untested. I rewrote the test suite to mock `window.matchMedia` (which is not available in jsdom by default), simulate OS preference changes, and verify that clicking the Dark mode button correctly updates local storage.

After the rewrite, coverage jumped to 91 percent.

The key mock that makes this all work is:

```javascript
const mockMatchMedia = jest.fn((query) => ({
  matches: false,
  media: query,
  addEventListener: jest.fn((event, callback) => {
    listeners[event] = callback;
  }),
  removeEventListener: jest.fn(),
}));

Object.defineProperty(window, "matchMedia", {
  writable: true,
  value: mockMatchMedia,
});
```

By capturing the `change` listener callback in a shared object, the test can then simulate an OS dark mode switch by calling the listener directly inside an `act()` block, without needing to fire actual browser events.

**The hero section redesign**

With the quality gate fixed, I moved on to Sprint 2: redesigning the Greeting component into a cinematic hero section.

The original layout was a simple two column flex row: text on the left, a photo on the right. Functional, but flat. Nothing about it communicated who I actually am as an engineer.

The goal for the new version was a split screen hero that answers three questions in under three seconds: who are you, what do you do, and why should I care?

Here is the exact React structure I used to build the new split screen layout:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import './Greeting.css';
import { greeting } from '../../portfolio';

export default function Greeting(props) {
  const theme = props.theme;

  return (
    <div className="greet-main" id="greeting">
      <div className="greeting-main">
        <div className="greeting-text-div">
          <div>
            <h1 className="greeting-text" style={{ color: theme.text }}>
              Hi, I'm <span className="hero-name gradient-text">{greeting.title}</span>
            </h1>
            <div className="hero-chips">
              {greeting.heroChips.map((chip) => (
                <span
                  key={chip.label}
                  className="chip"
                  style={{
                    borderColor: theme.secondaryText,
                    color: theme.text,
                    backgroundColor: theme.headerColor,
                  }}
                >
                  {chip.label}
                </span>
              ))}
            </div>
            <p className="greeting-text-p subTitle" style={{ color: theme.secondaryText }}>
              {greeting.subTitle}
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div className="greeting-image-div">
          <div className="hero-photo-ring">
            <img alt="Amrit profile" src={require('../../assets/images/profile.png')} className="hero-photo" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Gradient text**

Getting the name to render as a gradient required a CSS technique that is slightly unintuitive:

```css
.hero-name.gradient-text {
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, #388BFD, #C9D1D9);
  -webkit-background-clip: text;
  -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
  background-clip: text;
}
```

You apply a gradient as a background, then clip the background to the text shape, and make the text fill color transparent so the background shows through.

**Identity chips with staggered animations**

The three identity chips animate in sequentially when the page loads. Each chip is a span with a border, pill border radius, and a CSS animation. The sequential delay uses nth child CSS selectors:

```css
.chip {
  animation: slideInUp 0.5s ease-out backwards;
}
.chip:nth-child(1) { animation-delay: 0.1s; }
.chip:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: 0.2s; }
.chip:nth-child(3) { animation-delay: 0.3s; }

@keyframes slideInUp {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(20px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
}
```

The backwards fill mode ensures that before the animation starts, the chip stays invisible, so you do not see a flash of all chips appearing at once.

**Nepal flag inspired rotating photo ring**

This was my favourite part to build. Nepal has a flag with crimson red and blue. I used a conic gradient rotating infinitely around the profile photo to create a subtle animated border that nods to where I am from:

```css
.hero-photo-ring {
  border-radius: 50%;
  padding: 5px;
  background: conic-gradient(from 0deg, #DC143C, #003893, #DC143C);
  animation: rotateRing 10s linear infinite;
}

.hero-photo {
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: rotateRingReverse 10s linear infinite;
}

@keyframes rotateRing {
  from { transform: rotate(0deg); }
  to   { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}

@keyframes rotateRingReverse {
  from { transform: rotate(0deg); }
  to   { transform: rotate(-360deg); }
}
```

The counter rotation on the image element is essential. Without it, the photo itself spins inside the ring, which looks wrong. By counter rotating the image at the same speed, the photo appears stationary while only the ring border rotates.

**Polishing before merge**

After pushing the hero redesign to the draft PR, SonarCloud ran its static analysis and flagged a set of issues I needed to clean up before merging.

The first batch were all the same class of problem: every font family declaration in the CSS was missing a generic fallback. SonarCloud requires that custom font stacks always end with a generic family like sans serif so the browser has a safe fallback if the custom font fails to load. 

The second issue was using array indices as React keys. When you map over an array and use the loop index as the key prop, React cannot reliably track which element changed when items are reordered or removed. The fix is to use a stable, unique value from the data itself.

Both fixes are good practice regardless of what SonarCloud says. The font fallback matters for accessibility and resilience. The stable key prop matters for correctness when React reconciles the virtual DOM.

**What is next**

Sprint 2 is in draft PR on GitHub, passes all tests and the SonarCloud quality gate. Once it merges, Sprint 3 starts: adding a featured blogs preview section and a travel teaser directly to the home page.

